		<dialog
			id="shape-benchmark-dialog"
			class="rounded-md border border-amber-200/70 p-0 shadow-lg backdrop:bg-black/40 max-w-[min(92vw,48rem)] w-full"
		>
			<div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 px-3 py-2 border-b border-amber-200/70 bg-amber-50/90">
				<p class="text-sm font-medium">Silhouette benchmark (experimental)</p>
				<button
					type="button"
					id="shape-benchmark-dialog-close"
					class="text-sm px-2 py-0.5 rounded-sm bg-neutral-100 hover:bg-neutral-200"
				>
					Close
				</button>
			</div>
			<div id="shape-benchmark-body" class="p-3 max-h-[80vh] overflow-y-auto">
				<p class="text-xs text-neutral-600 mb-2">
					Binary-mask silhouette match: ranks by combined Score (default ~half IoU / half Chamfer) using amplified Euclidean edge distance plus a spread penalty (low σ preferred). Same rotation search and preprocess as before. Same OpenCV crop/bg step as Compare before masks; no ORB/SIFT in the bench.
					Hover any path with an id and use &quot;Silhouette bench&quot; in the preview — Check Floorplans is not required. Batch export runs the same benchmark for every path with an id across loaded SVGs, then downloads a CSV. Floorplans whose cropped aspect ratio is too far from the cutout&apos;s (default max span 2.5×) are skipped before scoring. Optional &quot;Flash preprocess in preview&quot; applies only to single-path runs from the preview (not batch). Duplicate benchmark files are deduped by content hash when loading.
					<strong class="font-medium">Preprocess cache:</strong> cropped floor images (same JS pipeline as silhouette IoU) are kept in memory; benchmark scores are also cached per path/cutout/max grid. Use export/import ZIP to persist fingerprints after a first pass—reloading the same floorplan files matches by content hash and skips expensive preprocessing.
					Optional <strong class="font-medium">unit reference JSON</strong> (<code class="text-[11px]">units[]</code> with <code class="text-[11px]">name</code> + <code class="text-[11px]">type</code>): for each path whose id contains a unit <code class="text-[11px]">name</code> (normalized), only benchmark images whose underscore tokens match the <code class="text-[11px]">type</code> rules are scored (see implementation). Paths with no matching unit still use the full image set.
				</p>
